/**
  @page guides-interfaces-dg Interfaces, traces, and discontinuous Galerkin
  @brief Working on facets: traces, jumps and averages, interface tagging, and DG formulations

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-ifdg-intro Introduction

  Many formulations live on *facets* rather than cells: Neumann data on
  the boundary, transmission conditions on material interfaces, and the
  entire discontinuous Galerkin (DG) family. This guide collects the
  facet-side vocabulary of the form language and its correct use.

  @section guides-ifdg-integrals The facet integral family

  | Class | Ranges over | Typical use |
  |-------|-------------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::BoundaryIntegral "BoundaryIntegral" | Exterior (codimension-1 boundary) facets | Neumann/Robin terms |
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::InterfaceIntegral "InterfaceIntegral" | Interior facets | DG coupling, transmission terms |
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::FaceIntegral "FaceIntegral" | All facets | Terms defined uniformly on faces |

  All accept attribute restriction via `.over(attr, ...)`, so a term can
  target a *tagged* subset of facets. Normals are provided by
  @ref Rodin::Variational::BoundaryNormal "BoundaryNormal" (outward on the
  boundary) and @ref Rodin::Variational::FaceNormal "FaceNormal" (a fixed
  orientation convention on interior faces — sign-sensitive terms must use
  it consistently on both sides).

  @section guides-ifdg-traces Traces: which side is meant?

  An @f$ H^1 @f$ function has a well-defined boundary trace, but on an
  *interior* facet a discontinuous quantity (a `P0` function, a gradient
  of a `P1` function, any DG field) has **two** one-sided values. Rodin
  requires you to disambiguate:

  - `f.traceOf(attr)` selects the trace *seen from* the cells with
    attribute `attr` — the tool for transmission problems where a
    coefficient jumps across a material interface;
  - if evaluation on an interior facet is attempted without a
    determined side, the library raises
    @ref Rodin::Variational::UndeterminedTraceDomainException
    "UndeterminedTraceDomainException" — this exception almost always
    means a missing `traceOf` on a coefficient in an interface term.

  Interfaces themselves are just *tagged facets*: use
  `mesh.trace({{attr1, attr2}, ifaceAttr})` to label the facets between
  two cell attributes, after computing the facet-cell connectivity (see
  @ref guides-connectivity).

  @section guides-ifdg-jump Jump and average

  The two facet operators of DG methods, defined for a quantity @f$ w @f$
  with one-sided values @f$ w^+, w^- @f$:

  @f[
    [\![ w ]\!] = w^+ - w^-, \qquad \{ w \} = \tfrac12 (w^+ + w^-),
  @f]

  available as @ref Rodin::Variational::Jump "Jump" and
  @ref Rodin::Variational::Average "Average":

  @code{.cpp}
  auto jf = Jump(f);       // f: any function with facet traces
  auto af = Average(f);
  @endcode

  Sanity properties worth internalizing (they are what the unit tests
  assert): the jump of any continuous function — a constant, or a
  conforming `P1`/`H1` grid function — is zero on every interior facet;
  the average of a continuous function equals its trace.

  @section guides-ifdg-dg Interior penalty DG in the form language

  The symmetric interior penalty (SIPG) discretization of
  @f$ -\Delta u = f @f$ reads

  @f[
    \sum_K \int_K \nabla u \cdot \nabla v
    \;-\; \int_{\mathcal F_i} \{\nabla u\}\cdot n \,[\![ v ]\!]
    \;-\; \int_{\mathcal F_i} \{\nabla v\}\cdot n \,[\![ u ]\!]
    \;+\; \int_{\mathcal F_i} \frac{\eta}{h} [\![ u ]\!]\,[\![ v ]\!]
    \;=\; \int_\Omega f v ,
  @f]

  written with `Integral` for the cell term and `InterfaceIntegral` for
  the three facet terms (plus `BoundaryIntegral` analogues to impose
  Dirichlet data weakly, Nitsche-style). Structural facts the mathematics
  imposes:

  - the **penalty scaling** @f$ \eta / h @f$ with @f$ \eta @f$ large
    enough (growing with the polynomial degree) is what makes the form
    coercive — omitting or under-sizing it is the standard DG
    instability, and it manifests as an indefinite system, not a crash;
  - the two consistency terms must be *transposes* of each other for
    symmetry (SIPG); flipping one sign gives the nonsymmetric NIPG
    variant with different penalty requirements;
  - assembled DG systems are not SPD in general — see
    @ref guides-solvers for appropriate solvers.

  See `examples/DG` for a starting point, and the `Jump`/`Average` unit
  tests (`tests/unit/Rodin/Variational/JumpTest.cpp`, `AverageTest.cpp`)
  for the precise operational semantics.

  @section guides-ifdg-seealso See also

  - @ref guides-boundary-conditions — strong (eliminated) boundary
    conditions, as opposed to the weak impositions here
  - @ref guides-connectivity — computing the facet incidences these
    integrals require
  - @ref guides-variational — the general problem-composition machinery
*/
